  "infoTextBinaryClockBcd": "Seconds since midnight in binary representation: each decimal digit of hours, minutes and seconds is shown as a separate binary column (BCD = Binary-Coded Decimal), as seen on the Wikipedia binary clock article.",
  "infoTextBinaryClockColumns": "Seconds since midnight in binary representation: each column shows hours, minutes, or seconds in base-2. Read top bit as most significant.",
  "infoTextBinaryClockString": "Seconds since midnight in compact binary representation: hours:minutes:seconds in base-2, e.g. 10:110000:10111.",
  "infoTextDate": "The current calendar date in the selected timezone.",
  "infoTextDateTime": "The current date and time in the selected timezone, combined in one value. Useful for copying a complete timestamp to the clipboard.",
  "infoTextDayPercent": "Percentage of the day elapsed in the selected timezone. Resets to 0 at midnight.",
  "infoTextDaySecond": "Seconds elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone. Resets to 0 at midnight.",
  "infoTextDecimalTime": "Decimal time, in use for a few years at the end of the 18th century in France, divides the day into 10 decimal hours. An hour has 100 decimal minutes, a minute 100 decimal seconds.",
  "infoTextDoomsdayClock": "The Doomsday Clock is a symbol representing how close humanity is to self-destruction. Maintained by the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists. Currently set to 85 seconds to midnight (as of Jan 2026).",
  "infoTextGmst": "Greenwich Mean Sidereal Time (GMST) – the hour angle of the vernal equinox at Greenwich. Used by astronomers to determine which part of the sky is overhead.",
  "infoTextGps": "GPS time counts seconds since 1980-01-06 00:00:00 UTC and does not include leap seconds. Currently, in May 2026, 18 seconds ahead of UTC. The last leap second was added on 2016-12-31 at 23:59:60 UTC.",
  "infoTextHexadecimalTime": "Seconds since midnight, shown in base 16 (hexadecimal), grouped in pairs for readability.",
  "infoTextJulianDate": "Julian Date counts days since noon on January 1, 4713 BC (-4712-01-01 12:00:00 UT). The standard time reference in astronomy for calculating intervals between events.",
  "infoTextLmst": "Local Mean Sidereal Time (LMST) – the hour angle of the vernal equinox at your location. Derived from GMST by adding your longitude. Used by astronomers to determine which objects are currently observable.",
  "infoTextModifiedJulianDate": "Modified Julian Date = JD − 2,400,000.5 (1858-11-17 00:00:00 UT). Starts at midnight rather than noon, and has smaller numbers. Introduced by the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory in 1957 to record the orbit of Sputnik.",
  "infoTextModifiedJulianDate2000": "Modified Julian Date 2000 = JD – 2,451,544.5 or MJD – 51,544 (2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC). Used in geodesy, geophysics, and spaceflight to simplify calculations with smaller numbers by shifting the reference to the beginning of the year 2000.",
  "infoTextNewEarthTime": "New Earth Time (or NET), also called degree time, splits a day into 360 NET degrees, where each NET degree has 60 NET minutes and each NET minute 60 NET seconds. UTC is the only time zone.",
  "infoTextOctalTime": "Seconds since midnight, shown in base 8 (octal).",
  "infoTextSevenSegmentTime": "The current time in the selected timezone in alarm clock design.",
  "infoTextSwatchBeats": "Swatch Internet Time divides the day into 1000 .beats. There are no time zones — the whole world shares the same .beat. Based on UTC+1 (Biel Mean Time).",
  "infoTextTai": "International Atomic Time (TAI) counts seconds since 1977-01-01 00:00:00 UTC and does not include leap seconds. Currently, in May 2026, 37 seconds ahead of UTC. The last leap second was added on 2016-12-31 at 23:59:60 UTC.",
  "infoTextTime": "The current time in the selected timezone.",
  "infoTextUnixSeconds": "Seconds elapsed since the Unix epoch: 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC.",
